    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_183ec6b3",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "text_chunk_1",
      "label": "Text: 'some people say religion'",
      "description": "This text introduces a common phrase or belief that religion is often described as a 'labor of love' — implying it is noble, selfless, or deeply personal. This phrase is a cultural reference to how religion is sometimes portrayed in media or public discourse as a positive, voluntary, and emotionally fulfilling endeavor. The meme uses this phrase to set up a contrast with the visual below, which challenges this notion.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a5d3d698"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_92fa5d4d",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "text_chunk_2",
      "label": "Text: 'is a labor of love'",
      "description": "This text completes the phrase 'some people say religion is a labor of love,' which is a common cultural trope used to describe religious devotion as a self-sacrificing, heartfelt, and often difficult commitment. The meme uses this phrase to frame the contrast with the image of a man being physically restrained while carrying a cross, implying that religious practice can be coercive or burdensome — thus mocking the idea of it being a 'labor of love.'",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a5d3d698"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_cc52137b",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "juxtaposed_religious_scene",
      "label": "Juxtaposed Religious Scene",
      "description": "The scene depicts a man carrying a cross while being escorted by police, which is a visual reference to the Passion of Christ, specifically the crucifixion narrative. The cross is a central symbol in Christianity, representing sacrifice and suffering. The juxtaposition of this religious symbol with police presence implies coercion or forced participation, which is used to critique or mock the idea that religious devotion is inherently noble or voluntary. This scene relies on cultural knowledge of Christian iconography and the historical context of religious persecution or forced religious practice.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a5d3d698"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_4dd96b12",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "anti_semitism",
      "label": "Anti-Semitism",
      "description": "The meme implicitly references anti-Semitic stereotypes by mocking religious devotion as a 'labor of love,' which can be interpreted as a disparaging portrayal of religious practice. This framing aligns with historical anti-Semitic tropes that portray religious practices as coercive, dangerous, or burdensome — such as the 'puppet master' imagery or the idea that religious devotion is inherently problematic. The meme’s intent to ridicule religious devotion can be seen as a form of religious practice mockery, which falls under the broader category of anti-Semitism in its historical context.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a5d3d698"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_11a9a23d",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "religious_practice_mockery",
      "label": "Religious Practice Mockery",
      "description": "The meme uses the juxtaposition of a man carrying a cross while being escorted by police to mock the idea that religious devotion is a 'labor of love.' This is a form of religious practice mockery, which is a common trope in anti-Semitic or anti-religious rhetoric. The meme implies that religious devotion can be coercive or burdensome, which is a disparaging stereotype that can be used to stigmatize religious individuals or practices. This mockery is not targeted at any specific protected group but is a generalization that can be harmful to religious communities.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a5d3d698"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_fd90b2ef",
      "@type": "EmotionExpression",
      "instance_name": "mockery",
      "label": "mocking emotion",
      "description": "The emotion expressed is mockery, conveyed through the juxtaposition of the actor John Malkovich's neutral, sophisticated expression with the image of a man being physically restrained while carrying a cross, combined with the text 'some people say religion is a labor of love.' This contrast is intended to ridicule the notion of religious devotion as noble or selfless, implying it is instead burdensome or coercive. The overall tone is satirical and critical, aiming to undermine the perceived value of religious practice.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a5d3d698"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_ed99791c",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_1",
      "label": "Religious devotion is mocked as a 'labor of love' through the visual of a man being escorted by police while carrying a cross",
      "description": "The text 'some people say religion is a labor of love' is juxtaposed with the visual of a man carrying a cross while being escorted by police. This creates an analogical mapping where the textual concept of 'labor of love' (implying voluntary, noble sacrifice) is contrasted with the visual depiction of coercion or forced participation, thereby mocking the notion that religious devotion is inherently selfless or voluntary. The visual material (the man with the cross and police) is mapped onto the textual phrase to illustrate the meme's critical stance on religious practice.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a5d3d698"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_00b98c1e",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the juxtaposed religious scene",
      "description": "The user is projected onto the juxtaposed religious scene (juxtaposed_religious_scene) where a man is carrying a cross while being escorted by police. This scene is contrasted with the text 'some people say religion is a labor of love,' implying the user is being invited to view religious devotion through the lens of coercion or burden, rather than voluntary sacrifice. The projection is implicit, as the meme does not use explicit deictic pronouns like 'you' or 'me,' but the user is expected to identify with the critical perspective presented by the juxtaposition.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a5d3d698"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_a5996c36",
      "@type": "ToxicityAssessment",
      "instance_name": "toxicity_1",
      "label": "Harmful",
      "description": "The meme juxtaposes a photo of actor John Malkovich with a photo of a man carrying a cross while being escorted by police, with the text \"some people say religion is a labor of love.\" The intent is to mock or ridicule the idea that religious devotion is noble or selfless, by contrasting it with the image of a man being physically restrained by authorities while carrying a cross, implying that religious practice can be burdensome or even dangerous. This framing can be seen as harmful as it potentially stigmatizes religious individuals or practices by suggesting they are inherently problematic or require coercion, which may contribute to negative perceptions of religion or religious people. The meme does not target any specific protected group with hate or abuse, but it does present a negative and potentially misleading portrayal of religious devotion, which can be harmful to religious communities or individuals who may feel misrepresented or attacked.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a5d3d698"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
